WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
When management frameworks are bypassed or
poorly implemented,

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.820
the research environment becomes
vulnerable to scientific misconduct.

3
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.800
The European Photoconduct identifies
baseline infractions.

4
00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.040
Fabrication involves making up data
entirely.

5
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.300
Falsification is the manipulation of
research materials or processes,

6
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.600
including the unjustified omission of
contradictory results.

7
00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:26.380
Plagiarism is the appropriation of another
person's ideas or work without

8
00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:28.420
providing proper original credit.

9
00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:32.140
Modern misconduct has expanded past these
initial categories.

10
00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:36.720
Researchers now face complex systemic
manipulations that exploit the

11
00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:41.120
publication ecosystem. One destructive
practice is known as salami

12
00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:45.700
publications. This occurs when a
researcher deliberately chops up a single

13
00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:50.560
cohesive set of research results into
multiple smaller papers to artificially

14
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.480
inflate their publication count.

15
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.160
Another issue is the hidden use of AI or
automated text tools to draft

16
00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.540
publications or generate content without
explicit disclosure to publishers and

17
00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:06.100
readers. Other predatory behaviors include
knowingly publishing in paper mills

18
00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:11.020
or predatory journals, attending dubious
conferences, and self-plagiarism,

19
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.640
republishing your own earlier work without
citation.

20
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.540
Misconduct can also be collaborative.

21
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:20.180
This includes manipulating authorship to
misrepresent a researcher's

22
00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.440
involvement, or participating in citation
cartels, where authors collude to

23
00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.380
review and cite each other's work.

24
00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:31.120
Modern scientific misconduct typically
stems from the strategic exploitation

25
00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:33.096
of poorly managed data life systems.
